<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <section xml:id="sec-section-1-5">
      <title>Section 05 - Properties of the Dot Product in 2 and 3 Dimensions</title>

<objectives>
  <p><em>Learning Objectives</em></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Calculate a dot product.</li>
    <li>Use properties of the dot product.</li>
    <li>Determine a vector projection.</li>
  </ul>
</objectives>
      
<subsection xml:id="subsec-calculate-dot-product">
  <title>Calculate a Dot Product</title>

  <p>
    The dot product of two vectors is computed by multiplying corresponding
    components and adding the results.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-dot-product">
    <title>Dot Product</title>

    <p>
      In two dimensions, let
      <m>\mathbf{u}=\langle u_1,\,u_2\rangle</m> and
      <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle v_1,\,v_2\rangle</m>.
      The dot product of <m>\mathbf{u}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is
    </p>

    <me>
      \mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}
      =
      u_1v_1+u_2v_2.
    </me>

    <p>
      In three dimensions, let
      <m>\mathbf{u}=\langle u_1,\,u_2,\,u_3\rangle</m> and
      <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle v_1,\,v_2,\,v_3\rangle</m>.
      The dot product of <m>\mathbf{u}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is
    </p>

    <me>
      \mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}
      =
      u_1v_1+u_2v_2+u_3v_3.
    </me>
  </definition>

  <p><xref ref="dot-product-figure"/> below shows how the dot product can also be understood component-by-component,
  by pairing horizontal and vertical contributions separately before adding them together.</p>

  <figure xml:id="dot-product-figure">
<title>Dot Product, Piece by Piece</title>
  <image source="images/ch01/DotProduct.png">
<shortdescription>
A visual breakdown showing how horizontal and vertical vector components pair and add to form the
dot product.
</shortdescription>
  </image>
<caption>
Diagram illustrating the dot product by adding the products of corresponding vector components.
</caption>
</figure>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-dot-product-properties">
  <title>Use Properties of the Dot Product</title>

  <p>
    To simplify and manipulate dot products algebraically, we rely on a small
    collection of fundamental properties that govern how the dot product
    behaves.
  </p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-dot-product-properties">
  <title>Properties of the Dot Product</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>\mathbf{u}</m>, <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, and <m>\mathbf{w}</m> be vectors, and
      let <m>k</m> be a scalar. Then:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <emph>Commutative.</emph>
          The order of vectors in a dot product does not change the result.
        </p>
        <me>
          \mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{v}\cdot\mathbf{u}
        </me>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <emph>Distributive.</emph>
          The dot product distributes over vector addition.
        </p>
        <me>
          \mathbf{u}\cdot\lbrack \mathbf{v}+\mathbf{w}\rbrack
          =
          \mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}
          +
          \mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{w}
        </me>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <emph>Associative (Scalar).</emph>
          Scalars may be factored in or out of a dot product.
        </p>
        <me>
          k\lbrack \mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}\rbrack
          =
          \lbrack k\mathbf{u}\rbrack \cdot \mathbf{v}
          =
          \mathbf{u}\cdot \lbrack k\mathbf{v}\rbrack
        </me>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <emph>Magnitude.</emph>
          The dot product of a vector with itself equals the square of its magnitude.
        </p>
        <me>
          \mathbf{v}\cdot\mathbf{v}=|\mathbf{v}|^2
        </me>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
</theorem>


  <example xml:id="ex-using-dot-product-properties">
    <p>
      Simplify the expression
      <m>
        (2\mathbf{u} - \mathbf{v}) \cdot (\mathbf{u} + \mathbf{v})
      </m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Apply the distributive and scalar properties of the dot product.
    </p>

    <me>
      (2\mathbf{u} - \mathbf{v}) \cdot (\mathbf{u} + \mathbf{v})
      =
      2\mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{u}
      +
      2\mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{v}
      -
      \mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{u}
      -
      \mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{v}.
    </me>
  </example>

  <worksheet xml:id="dot-product-properties-examples">
  <title>In-Class Examples</title>

  <exercise xml:id="dot-product-properties-ex-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbf{u}=\langle -3,\,2\rangle</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle 9,\,-1\rangle</m>.
        Before computing, predict whether
        <m>\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}</m> should be positive, negative, or zero.
        Then compute <m>\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="dot-product-properties-ex-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbf{u}=\langle -2,\,4,\,-1\rangle</m>,
        <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle 5,\,-3,\,-2\rangle</m>, and
        <m>\mathbf{w}=\langle 0,\,-1,\,-6\rangle</m>.
        Compute
        <m>(\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{w})\mathbf{v}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="dot-product-properties-ex-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>\mathbf{v}\cdot\mathbf{v}=8</m>.
        Find <m>|\mathbf{v}|</m>.
        What does <m>\mathbf{v}\cdot\mathbf{v}</m> represent geometrically?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="dot-product-properties-ex-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}=9</m>.
        Find
        <m>\dfrac{4}{\lbrack \mathbf{v}\cdot\mathbf{u} \rbrack^2}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>

</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="subsec-dot-product-exercises">
  <title>Exercises on the Dot Product</title>

  <worksheet xml:id="dot-product-practice">
    <title>Practice Problems</title>

    <exercise xml:id="dot-product-practice-1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine whether each statement is <em>true</em> or <em>false</em>.
          Justify your answer or provide a counterexample.
        </p>

        <ol marker="A.">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}-\mathbf{v}\cdot\mathbf{u}=0</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              For any scalar <m>c</m>,
              <m>|c\mathbf{v}|=c|\mathbf{v}|</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If
              <m>
                |\mathbf{u}|^2+|\mathbf{v}|^2
                =
                |\mathbf{u}+\mathbf{v}|^2
              </m>,
              then <m>\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}=0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="dot-product-practice-2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <m>\mathbf{u}=\langle -1,\,4,\,6\rangle</m>,
          <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle 2,\,8,\,5\rangle</m>, and
          <m>\mathbf{w}=\langle 0,\,-1,\,-2\rangle</m>.
          Find each of the following.
        </p>

        <ol marker="a.">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\mathbf{u}\cdot(\mathbf{v}+\mathbf{w})</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>|\mathbf{v}|^2</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>-2(\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{w})</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="dot-product-practice-3">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <m>\mathbf{u}=\langle 1,\,3,\,-5\rangle</m> and
          <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle -4,\,0,\,3\rangle</m>.
          Compute <m>\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="dot-product-practice-4">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Answer each question briefly. Justifications should focus on
          interpretation and meaning, not computation.
        </p>

        <ol marker="a.">
          <li>
            <p>
              What kind of quantity is the dot product: a vector or a scalar?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Can the dot product of two nonzero vectors be zero?
              What would this indicate geometrically?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Can the dot product of two vectors be negative?
              What might that suggest about their relative directions?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Without computing, determine whether the dot product of
              <m>\langle 1,\,2,\,-3\rangle</m> and
              <m>\langle 4,\,-1,\,6\rangle</m>
              should be positive, negative, or zero.
              Explain.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

  </worksheet>
</subsection>

    </section>
